# Türkçe metnin birebir İngilizce çevirisi (24 Kasım 2026). Alan adları İngilizce sözlükle aynı.
# Türkçesi sonradan genişleyip İngilizcesi eski kalan alanlar. build_en.py bunları asıl metnin üzerine yazar.
EN_FIX = {}
def _f(i, **k): EN_FIX.setdefault(i, {}).update(k)

# --- güvenlik ---
_f('andiroba', safety='Generally safe on the skin; not to be swallowed. The oil can cause skin irritation and allergic rash; test it on a small area before applying it widely. Not to be applied to open wounds or around the eyes. There are no safety data in pregnancy or for babies; in the Amazon rubbing it on babies as a mosquito repellent is a traditional use, but there is no safety study supporting it.')
_f('ballibaba', safety='No serious toxicity or adverse effect has been reported for white dead-nettle, and even mistaken for a nettle it does not sting. But **there is no human safety data either** — nothing supports regular use in pregnancy, lactation or children, so making a habit of it is not advised. Allergy in the mint family is rare but possible. Where it is gathered matters: it grows on roadsides and hedgerows, and should not be collected where exhaust or pesticide may have settled. Safety level: "low risk".')
_f('camucamu', safety='The fruit is safe as food. Its vitamin C content is very high: in concentrated powder form the daily intake easily rises, and too much vitamin C means diarrhoea, heartburn and, in susceptible people, a risk of kidney stones. Because it increases iron absorption, care is needed in conditions with iron overload. The safety of the powder form in pregnancy is unknown.')
_f('ejderkani', safety='Not to be used in pregnancy: folk use as an abortifacient in the first trimester is on record. No data for breastfeeding. In rats no toxic findings appeared up to a single 2000 mg/kg dose or 1500 mg/kg daily for 28 days. The real risk is adulteration: samples collected from Yemen’s own markets have been found partly or wholly substituted with other substances, and according to the pharmacopoeia the authentic source of “dragon’s blood” is not this tree but the palm Daemonorops draco. Which plant a product on sale comes from cannot be told without laboratory analysis.')
_f('gazalboynuzu', safety='⚠️ **It carries cyanogenic glycosides** (linamarin, lotaustralin). The amount varies genetically from plant to plant, so "how much is safe" has no answer for a herbal tea. For that reason it is not recommended as a drink, and even in animal feeding the amount of fresh herb is kept measured. Because an effect on the central nervous system was shown in an animal model, care is needed for anyone on sedatives. No data for pregnancy, lactation or children. Safety level: "caution".')
_f('ihlamur', safety='No known side effects or drug interactions; the only definite barrier is an allergy to linden flowers. The EMA monograph does not recommend it in pregnancy and breastfeeding for lack of sufficient data, and it is not used for colds in children under four, or for tension in children under twelve. If a cold lasts longer than a week, or comes with shortness of breath, high fever or purulent phlegm, tea is not enough.')
_f('kapari', safety='The pickled buds are safe as food. The brine is high in salt: people on a salt restriction need to count it. There are no human safety data for medicinal use of the root bark; it should not be used in this form in pregnancy.')
_f('kimyon', safety='Safe as a spice. The safety of regular, medicine-like use above spice amounts is unknown; it should not be used in such amounts in pregnancy. There are findings that it may lower blood sugar; people on diabetes medicines should monitor their levels. Cross-allergy may occur in people allergic to the carrot family (celery, carrot, anise).')
_f('kizilcik', safety='The fruit is safe as food. Because the stone is hard, it is not given whole to small children. Because it is very sour, it may worsen symptoms in people with reflux. There are no safety data for medicinal use of the leaves and bark.')
_f('yabaniigde', safety='The ripe fruit is safe as food. Unripe fruit is astringent and dries the mouth, and too much upsets the stomach. The seeds are a choking risk for small children. There are no safety data for medicinal use of the leaves and bark.')

# --- hazırlama ---
_f('abuta', prep='The root is boiled; in the Amazon usually for a long time, until a dark decoction is obtained. There is no accepted dose. Because it may act on the uterine muscle it is not used in pregnancy; because it may lower blood pressure, people on blood-pressure medicines need to monitor their readings.')
_f('achiote', prep='The seeds are crushed into a powder, or steeped in hot oil so the colour passes into the oil; this is how it is used as a food colouring and body paint. It is safe as food, in amounts suited to the dish. There is no accepted dose for any medicinal purpose.')
_f('akadacayi', prep='A bundle of dried leaves is tied, its tip lit, the flame put out and the smoke wafted — this is its use as incense. It is not prepared for drinking. Indoors the smoke irritates the airways; it should not be burned in a room where someone has asthma. ⚠️ White sage is a sacred plant for the Indigenous peoples of California and is under commercial harvesting pressure; if you buy it, ask where it comes from.')
_f('alic', prep='The flowering twigs and leaves are steeped. The amount the EMA monograph gives for herbal tea: 1–2 g of chopped herbal substance in 150 ml of boiling water, 3–4 cups a day, not exceeding 6 g a day. The monograph does not recommend use under 18. If symptoms last longer than four weeks, see a doctor; if there is swelling of the ankles, pain spreading to the arm, upper abdomen or neck, or shortness of breath, not tea but a doctor without delay.')
_f('banotu', prep='Preparing and using it is not recommended; this page deliberately gives no recipe. The reason is not the dose but that a dose cannot be controlled: the amount of hyoscyamine and scopolamine varies many-fold from plant to plant, with the season and with the part gathered, so there is no such thing as a safe amount at home. Historical ointment and painkiller uses are on record, and all carry the same risk.')
_f('chanca', prep='The dried aerial parts are steeped. Despite its widespread traditional use there is no accepted dose; the extract amounts used in clinical studies differ from one another, and tea and extract are not interchangeable. Because it may lower blood sugar and blood pressure, people on these medicines need to monitor their levels.')
_f('chuchuhuasi', prep='The bark is steeped in alcohol for several days; in the Amazon it is mostly prepared with sugar-cane spirit. There is no accepted dose. Because the preparation contains alcohol, all the risks of alcohol apply: it is not used in pregnancy, in liver disease or by people who have stopped drinking.')
_f('cucepalmiye', prep='It is not prepared as a tea: the compounds held responsible for the effect dissolve in fat, not in water. The forms accepted in the EMA assessment are soft extracts — 160 mg twice a day (hexane extract), or 320 mg once a day (ethanol extract). For adults only; if there is fever, cramps or blood in the urine, a doctor is needed rather than a product, because these symptoms may be a sign of something other than prostate enlargement.')
_f('guayusa', prep='The leaves are boiled for a long time; in the Amazon it is drunk together early in the morning. Because it contains caffeine, think of the amount as you would coffee — there is no accepted daily dose, but people sensitive to caffeine, those with heart rhythm problems, anxiety or insomnia, and pregnant women should count their total caffeine intake. Drunk in the evening, it disturbs sleep.')
_f('kankoku', prep='Use is not recommended; this page gives no recipe. The sanguinarine in the rhizome causes vomiting and visual disturbance when taken by mouth and tissue death when applied to the skin. It is the main ingredient of the mixtures sold online as “black salve”, and the wound it leaves heals with a scar; the claim that it “draws out” tumours is not true — what it does is burn the tissue.')
_f('kantuta', prep='The flowers and leaves are steeped. In the Andes the plant’s weight is ceremonial rather than medicinal: it is the national flower of Peru and Bolivia and is placed on graves and offerings. There is no accepted dose for medicinal use and no safety study — this page gives no amount.')
_f('kapari', prep='The buds are picked and dried, then their bitterness is removed by salting or keeping them in vinegar brine; without this they are not edible. The pickle is used in moderation in food. There is no accepted dose for medicinal use of the root bark.')
_f('kimyon', prep='The seeds are roasted and ground; roasting releases the essential oil, so this is the step that really gives the scent. For indigestion, a dessertspoon of seeds is steeped in a cup of boiling water. There is no accepted dose for regular, medicine-like use above spice amounts.')
_f('kinin', prep='Preparing it yourself is not recommended. The amount of quinine in the bark varies with the tree and the age of the bark, so the amount taken in a home-made decoction cannot be known — and the dose at which quinine is toxic is close to the dose at which it treats. Malaria is treated with prescription medicine. The quinine in tonic water is far below a medicinal dose and is not a treatment.')
_f('meyan', prep='The root is soaked or boiled in water. The amount the EMA monograph gives for digestive complaints: 1.5–2 g of chopped root in 150 ml of boiling water, 2–4 cups a day after meals (3–8 g a day), not exceeding four weeks; if symptoms last more than two weeks, see a doctor. For cough, 1.5–4.5 g per dose, 2–3 times a day; if it lasts more than a week, see a doctor. The monograph does not recommend use under 18, in pregnancy or while breastfeeding.')
_f('muna', prep='Fresh or dried sprigs are steeped; in the Andean highlands it is drunk after meals and against altitude sickness. Drinking it in moderation as a tea is considered safe, but there is no accepted daily dose. The essential oil is a different matter: it is rich in pulegone, harmful to the liver, and not to be drunk.')
_f('pauarco', prep='The inner bark of the tree (lapacho) is boiled; the outer bark and wood are not used, as the compounds considered active are in the inner bark. There is no accepted dose, and the contents of products on the market do not match one another — in some analyses the expected component was not found at all. In large amounts it can cause nausea, vomiting and bleeding.')
_f('rezene', prep='The seeds are lightly crushed and steeped — uncrushed, the essential oil does not pass into the water. The amount in the EMA monograph for sweet fennel (var. dulce): 1.5 g of herbal substance in 250 ml of boiling water for 15 minutes, 3 cups a day, 4.5 g a day. The monograph does not recommend the tea under four years of age (for bloating and cough), or under twelve for period pain, and says it should not be used for longer than two weeks.')
_f('sachainchi', prep='The seeds must always be roasted before eating; raw seeds and leaves contain alkaloids and saponins, which roasting largely removes. The oil is extracted by cold pressing and is not used for frying: being rich in omega-3, it breaks down with heat, so it goes into salads and cold dishes.')
_f('sangre', prep='The red resin that oozes from the trunk is applied directly to the skin; as it dries it leaves a thin layer. There is no accepted dose for the form taken by mouth and safety data are limited — this page gives no amount for internal use. For external use it is not applied to open wounds or around the eyes.')
_f('sanpedro', prep='The stem is sliced and boiled for hours. This page gives no amount: because the mescaline content varies with the column, its age and the conditions it grew in, two drinks prepared the same way from the same plant will not be equally strong. In Türkiye mescaline is regulated as a narcotic; keeping the plant as an ornamental and preparing its extract are two different things.')
_f('sinekmantari', prep='Preparing and using it is not recommended; this page deliberately gives no recipe. The amount of ibotenic acid and muscimol varies from mushroom to mushroom, even between two mushrooms in the same ring. The main danger is separate: when gathering it can be confused with deadly Amanita species, and that mistake is irreversible.')
_f('tatula', prep='Preparing and using it is not recommended; this page deliberately gives no recipe. The amount of atropine and scopolamine in the seeds varies many-fold from plant to plant, so there is no safe range of “a small amount”. It grows wild in Türkiye and poisoning cases are seen every year; most come from young people who do not know the plant trying it.')
_f('toe', prep='Preparing and using it is not recommended; this page deliberately gives no recipe. The tropane alkaloids in its flowers, leaves and seeds vary greatly from plant to plant and with the season; the gap between a lethal amount and an effective amount is narrow. In Türkiye it is sold as an ornamental: it should not be kept where children and pets can reach it.')
_f('unadegato', prep='The bark is boiled for 20–30 minutes. Traditional amounts in the literature do not agree: one record boils 20 g of chopped root bark in 1 litre of water for 45 minutes; another boils two spoons of bark in 1.5 litres for 30 minutes and drinks half a glass three times a day. The WHO monograph gives 300–500 mg two to three times a day for capsules. The EMA assessment report could not establish a monograph for Europe; so there is no single commonly accepted dose.')
_f('yacon', prep='The tuber is peeled and eaten raw; its juice is boiled down to a thick syrup. Its sweetness comes from fructo-oligosaccharides, which are not digested in the gut: too much causes wind, bloating and diarrhoea, so start with a small amount. The leaf tea is a separate thing — its long-term safety is unknown, and this page gives no amount for the leaf.')

# --- araştırmalar ---
_f('hibiskus', science='Several controlled trials and meta-analyses have shown that hibiscus tea lowers blood pressure slightly. The studies are small and short. Although it is sold as a slimming product, no effect on weight has been shown: a 2024 meta-analysis combining six randomised trials found no significant difference in weight, body mass index or waist circumference.')
_f('kirmizikaraagac', science='The inner bark is rich in mucilage that turns jelly-like with water and coats the surface of the throat and stomach. The US FDA accepts slippery elm as an over-the-counter throat soother. Studies on digestive complaints have tested the plant not alone but within mixtures of several plants, so how much of the observed improvement comes from slippery elm is not known.')
_f('palosanto', science='Rich in limonene and α-terpineol. An insect-repellent effect has been seen in small studies; a 2025 study suggests the essential oil may act on stored-grain pest insects through GABA and octopamine receptors. There are also laboratory studies in which the essential oil was tested as an additive in a drug gel against skin fungi; these are at the level of cell and skin models and do not mean use in humans.')
_f('tatliot', science='Its sweet, vanilla-like scent comes from coumarin; how coumarin is produced in this plant was shown in 1960 by an isotope-labelling study. There are no human studies of its medicinal use. On the other hand, a 2016 laboratory study measured that the plant’s steam distillate deters mosquitoes from biting and isolated the compounds responsible — so the habit of the Flathead communities in Montana and the Blackfoot in Alberta of carrying the braid among clothes or burning it has a measurable counterpart.')

_f('aguaymanto', prep='The fruit is eaten fresh or dried.')
_f('sumak', safety='Safe as a spice. Cross-allergy may occur in people allergic to cashew and pistachio.')
_f('sarimsak', safety='Safe as food. High doses may increase the effect of blood thinners; it should be stopped before surgery.')

