_f('sassafras', habitat='On forest edges, abandoned fields and in thickets of eastern North America.')
_f('yuksukotu', habitat='In Türkiye species such as D. ferruginea and D. lanata grow wild; the red-flowered D. purpurea, however, is not wild in Türkiye and is grown in gardens as an ornamental (Baytop).')
_f('huayruro', habitat='In the lowland forests of the Amazon, from sea level up to 500 metres [1].')
_f('akgunluk', habitat='B. sacra grows on the dry, rocky slopes of Oman, Yemen and Somalia; B. serrata in the dry forests of India. Excessive resin tapping threatens some species.')
_f('kusuvezi', habitat='Recorded in the flora of Türkiye (Gökşin 1982, as cited by Macit et al. 2022); its fruit has been studied with samples collected from Bayburt (Çınar Topçu et al. 2025). Around 35 Sorbus species grow in the Caucasus and Türkiye.')
_f('ejderkani', habitat='In the world it grows naturally only on the island of Socotra. It is in the Vulnerable (VU) category of the IUCN Red List; this assessment dates from 2004 and has not been renewed since. Overgrazing, climate change and unsustainable harvesting keep it under pressure.')
_f('seytanpencesi', habitat='The Kalahari desert and its surroundings — Namibia, Botswana, South Africa. It grows on sandy, arid soils. It has no natural distribution in Türkiye; it is imported.')

# --- ilginç ---
_f('uzerlik', fact='The red dye obtained from its seeds has been used in traditional carpet and fabric dyeing.')
_f('rezene', fact='The word marathon comes from the plain near Athens full of fennel fields; Greek “marathon” means fennel.')
_f('unadegato', fact='It takes its name from the hook-shaped thorns on the vine’s branches; the plant climbs trees with these thorns.')
_f('hatmi', fact='The English name “marshmallow” comes from the plant being a relative of the mallow that grows at the edge of marshes.')
_f('zakkum', fact='Even using oleander branches as skewers or barbecue wood can lead to poisoning; care is needed at picnics.')
_f('sigirkuyrugu', fact='More than 200 Verbascum species grow in Türkiye, and most of them are endemic species found only in Türkiye.')
_f('kenevir', fact='Turkish “kendir” and “kenevir” come from the same root; the word kenevir also passed into European languages (cannabis, hemp) from this old root.')
_f('zerdecal', fact='It came to light that lead chromate paint had been added to cheap turmeric to brighten its colour; this led to lead poisonings in Bangladesh.')
_f('reishi', fact='Because it is rare in nature, it could once reach only palaces; almost all the reishi sold today is grown on sawdust or on logs.')
_f('yuksukotu', fact='It takes its name from its flowers, which resemble a thimble put on the finger; its German name is also Fingerhut, meaning “finger hat”.')
_f('mercimek', fact='All the lentil remains obtained by floating 1,950 litres of soil from Göbekli Tepe amount to half of a single seed — and with its diameter of 2.1 millimetres, this half seed falls within the range not of domesticated but of wild lentil.')

# --- karışma ---
_f('rezene', confusion='The same family has look-alikes such as the deadly poisonous hemlock (Conium maculatum). Fennel is recognised by its distinct aniseed smell; umbrella-flowered plants that do not carry this smell must never be gathered.')
_f('palosanto', confusion='Some of the products sold as palo santo are made from other Bursera species or from ordinary scented wood.')
_f('kinin', confusion='The name cinchona covers several Cinchona species, and their quinine levels differ from one another.')
_f('toe', confusion='It is confused with jimsonweed (Datura); both are deadly poisonous. Toé’s flowers hang downward, jimsonweed’s face upward.')
_f('akadacayi', confusion='Bundles of other Salvia or Artemisia may be sold on the market as white sage.')
_f('sumak', confusion='Some Rhus and Toxicodendron species grown as ornamentals in gardens irritate the skin. The fruit of spice sumac is red and hairy.')
_f('sigirkuyrugu', confusion='Because it has many endemic species, gathering the rare species is harmful from the point of view of conservation.')
_f('kenevir', confusion='Industrial hemp and varieties with a high THC content look alike; the difference can only be told in a laboratory.')
_f('yabanmersini', confusion='⚠️ There are three separate confusions. The first is the name: in Turkish "ayı üzümü" describes both this plant and Arctostaphylos uva-ursi, and their uses differ — uva-ursi is used for the urinary tract. The second is the species: three more plants of the same genus are on the shelf or in this catalog — the American blueberry (Vaccinium corymbosum) is large and pale inside, lingonberry (V. vitis-idaea) is red, and cranberry (V. macrocarpon) is used for the urinary tract. Bilberry is small and purple inside too; the monographs cover only V. myrtillus. The third is the form of preparation: the fresh and the dried fruit have separate monographs and different indications.')
_f('olmezcicek', confusion='It is confused with other species of the same genus: Helichrysum italicum (Italian everlasting), which grows around the Mediterranean, is sold in cosmetics and as an essential oil and is a different species; the EU monograph covers only H. arenarium. In Türkiye many Helichrysum species are gathered under the names "ölmez çiçek" and "altın otu". ⚠️ **Paper flower (Xeranthemum annuum), a separate genus, is also called "everlasting flower" in English**; it is a separate plant, has no TİTCK monograph and is in its own record.')

# --- kullanım (bölge, metin) ---
_f('meyan', uses=[["Anatolia", "In the southeast, licorice sherbet is drunk during Ramadan; it is considered cooling and kind to the stomach. The root is chewed against cough and heartburn."], ["Asia", "It is one of the most frequently used plants in Chinese medicine; it is added to prescriptions because it “brings the other herbs into harmony”."]])
_f('safran', uses=[["Anatolia", "Safranbolu takes its name from this plant; it has been grown around the town for centuries. It gives colour to zerde and to pilafs; among the people it is considered to bring joy and strengthen the heart."], ["Ancient Aegean", "The Akrotiri frescoes on Santorini (17th century BC) depict young women gathering saffron."]])
_f('sanpedro', uses=[["Andes", "On the northern coast and in the mountains of Peru, healers give the huachuma drink in “mesa” ceremonies that last all night. It is used to see the cause of an illness and to break sorcery."], ["History", "Stone carvings at Chavín de Huántar (1st millennium BC) include figures carrying this cactus; they point to a tradition of more than 3,000 years."]])
_f('koka', uses=[["Andes", "In Andean villages the leaf is held inside the cheek and chewed (chacchar, akulliku). It is used against altitude sickness (soroche), hunger and fatigue. It is at the centre of the offerings made to Pachamama; fortunes are read by watching how the leaves fall."], ["Peru today", "Mate de coca (coca tea) is served freely in hotels and cafés; it is the first drink offered to travellers arriving in Cusco."]])
_f('anason', uses=[["Anatolia", "It is the spice that gives rakı its scent; it is grown in fields in the Lakes Region and the Aegean. Among the people anise tea is drunk for wind, indigestion and cough; it is given to nursing mothers to increase their milk."], ["Mediterranean", "Greek ouzo, French pastis and the arak of the Levant are also flavoured with anise. In Rome, anise cakes are said to have been served at the end of great feasts to help digestion."]])
_f('pasiflora', uses=[["North America", "The Indigenous peoples of the southeastern US (such as the Cherokee) ate its fruit and used its root and leaves on wounds and swellings. In the region it is known as “maypop”."], ["Europe and today", "It reached Europe in the 17th century; today in Europe it is used in herbal medicines for anxiety and sleep problems."]])
_f('fitolakka', uses=[["North America", "Indigenous peoples and later settlers made dye and ink from its berries. In the southern states the young shoots are boiled two or three times, the water poured off, and eaten under the name “poke sallet”."], ["Europe and Türkiye", "It was brought to Europe in the 17th century as an ornamental and dye plant; in Portugal its berries are said to have been added to darken wine, and this to have been banned. It has also been recorded in Türkiye; escaping from gardens, it runs wild here and there."]])
_f('salviadivinorum', uses=[["Mexico (Mazatec)", "The Mazatec drink a mild tea prepared from a few fresh leaves for headache, fever, joint pain and general pain; they use the leaves externally for joint pain, swelling, insect stings and sore throat. Some also use it to treat animals."], ["Ritual", "The Mazatec regard the plant as a sacred being; they call it by the name “xka pastora”, which evokes the Virgin Mary. They grow it near their homes but hide it from view; the relationship with the plant is bound by detailed rules."]])
_f('mese', uses=[["Celts", "According to the Roman writer Pliny, the druids of Gaul held nothing more sacred than mistletoe and the oak on which it grows, and performed no rite without an oak branch. The mistletoe was cut on the sixth day of the moon by a priest dressed in white with a golden sickle and caught in a white cloth."], ["Galatia (Central Anatolia)", "Strabo writes that the 300-member council of the Celtic Galatians who settled in Central Anatolia met at a place called Drynemetum. Frazer reads this name as “sacred oak grove”."], ["Northern and Eastern Europe", "According to Frazer’s compilation the oak was the tree of Donar (Thor) among the Germans, of Perun among the Slavs and of Perkūnas among the Lithuanians — that is, of the thunder gods. In Novgorod a fire of oak wood was kept burning night and day for Perun."], ["Anatolia", "In Doğanşehir, Malatya, large oaks are called “ziyaret ağacı” (pilgrimage tree) or “ulu ağaç” (great tree); wishes are made and rags tied to them, meals are eaten beneath them, and they are not harmed. In Sorgun, Yozgat, a centuries-old grove of valonia oak has been preserved to this day thanks to the beliefs surrounding it."], ["European folk medicine", "The bark is used as an astringent for diarrhoea, inflammation inside the mouth and of the skin, and haemorrhoids; the European Medicines Agency (EMA) recognises these uses as traditional use."]])
_f('porsuk', uses=[["Britain and Ireland", "Very old yews are frequently found in early Christian churchyards, near prehistoric graves and sacred sites. The Fortingall yew in Scotland is estimated to be 2,000–5,000 years old, and the Defynnog yew in Wales over 3,000; the churches are thought to have been built on top of pre-Christian sacred places. In the Celtic, Greek and Roman worlds the yew is associated with death, rebirth and immortality."], ["Rome", "Pliny mentions the yew as a tree of “gloomy and ominous appearance”; he writes that in Gaul wine flasks made from the wood of this tree killed those who drank from them."], ["Bursa", "In the village of Alpağut in Mustafakemalpaşa, green yew shoots used to be fed to animals too sick to recover, so that they would not suffer; this knowledge was passed down from father to son. In Dallıca, elders tell that the minaret of a wooden mosque was made of “gürdüç” wood."], ["Modern medicine", "The cancer drug paclitaxel (Taxol) was found in the bark of the Pacific yew in the 1960s. When trees had to be cut down for a single patient, production turned to semi-synthesis from a precursor obtained from the needles of the European yew; without cutting the tree, its renewable part could be used."]])
_f('badem', uses=[["Göbekli Tepe", "In layer III of Göbekli Tepe, among the remains obtained by floating 1,950 litres of soil, there are shell fragments belonging to the genus Prunus: 158 fragments, corresponding to an estimated 13.5 whole fruits. Part of the charcoal has also been identified as Prunus cf. amygdalus. So what was found is not the domesticated almond but its wild relative; the excavation’s archaeobotanist Reinder Neef writes that there is no evidence of domestication in any of the material. About nine tenths of the charcoal consists of wild pistachio and wild almond; this shows that the vegetation surrounding the hill was an “almond-pistachio steppe forest”."], ["Şanlıurfa — Suruç", "In the same province as Göbekli Tepe, in the villages of Suruç, almond fruit is eaten for diabetes; its local name is “beivf”."], ["Şırnak — İdil", "Fresh almond fruit is used for mouth sores, for eczema and to thicken the hair; its local names are “badem” and “behiv”."], ["Mardin — non-medicinal use", "The resin that seeps from the trunk of almond and eastern almond (Amygdalus orientalis) is used as glue: book pages that have come loose are stuck together with it. The trunk is also valued as firewood."]])
_f('ejderkani', uses=[["Socotra", "The tree grows naturally only on the island of Socotra, which belongs to Yemen. The resin is made to flow by cutting notches in the trunk and is then dried; the island’s people use it for skin, tooth, eye and digestive complaints. Centuries of harvesting have left countless wounds on the trees."], ["Arab and Islamic medicine", "In classical Arabic sources the resin appears under the name dem-i ahaveyn, that is, “blood of the two brothers”. Al-Asmaʿi around 800 and Ibn al-Baytar in 1248 mention it under the names “dragon’s blood” and “snake’s blood”. Ibn Sina’s Canon and al-Razi’s al-Hawi also list the drug: it was used for wounds, fractures, ulcers, diarrhoea and to stop bleeding."], ["Ancient Mediterranean", "The first text to mention the resin is the Periplus of around AD 60; it calls the drug “Indian cinnabar”. Dioscorides writes of it under the name kinnabari, as a substance “brought from Africa”."], ["Dye and lacquer", "Dragon’s blood was used in works of art of the 15th–19th centuries in gold lacquers, transparent glazes and reverse glass painting; this use has been shown by gas chromatography. Although it is often named as a colourant in historical sources, it is detected very rarely in actual works."]])
_f('antepfistigi', uses=[["Domestication in the Fertile Crescent", "Pistachio is a nut tree domesticated in the Fertile Crescent. In Türkiye the area around Gaziantep, Şanlıurfa and Siirt is the centre of production; the species’ first reference genome was also sequenced from a female individual of the Siirt cultivar."], ["Wild relatives in Anatolian folk medicine", "The Pistacia species whose folk-medicine use is documented in Anatolian ethnobotanical records are the wild ones, not pistachio itself. In Şırnak the branch and fruit of Pistacia eurycarpa (“bendek”, “kızvan”, “bıttım”) are boiled and used against constipation, haemorrhoids, stomach ache and stomach ulcer. In Mardin bıttım soap is made from the fruit of Pistacia khinjuk (“bıttım”, “kızvan”). Pistacia terebinthus, for its part, is known by the name menengiç, and its fruit and resin are used. No Anatolian folk-medicine record could be found for pistachio itself (Pistacia vera); its cultural weight lies in the kitchen and in trade."], ["Not found at Göbekli Tepe", "In layer III of Göbekli Tepe, fruit remains belonging to the genus Pistacia (124 fragments, an estimated 17 whole) and the largest share of the charcoal (3.14 g, 104 identifications) were found. But the charcoal was identified as Pistacia cf. atlantica: this is not pistachio but a wild Pistacia, that is, a menengiç/buttum species. The sentence “pistachio was found at Göbekli Tepe” is not supported by the source."]])
_f('mercimek', uses=[["Göbekli Tepe — half a seed", "At Göbekli Tepe, all the lentil remains coming from the flotation of 1,950 litres of soil amount to half of a single seed. Reinder Neef measured the diameter of this half seed: 2.1 millimetres, that is, within the range of wild lentil. Not domesticated lentil."], ["In Anatolian cuisine", "Lentil is one of the oldest and most widespread staple foods in Anatolia; its soup, köfte and pilaf are made in dozens of forms that change from region to region. Yet in none of the Turkish ethnobotany and folk-medicine papers we searched does lentil appear as a folk remedy: there is no record in the Suruç, Şırnak, Mardin and Develi surveys. The weight of lentil lies in the kitchen, not in folk medicine."], ["At the beginning of agriculture", "Lentil is one of the first legumes domesticated together with cereals in the Fertile Crescent. Its wild ancestor is Lens culinaris subsp. orientalis. Archaeobotanical studies show that the domestication of legumes was slower than that of cereals; the increase in seed size took centuries."]])
_f('arpa', uses=[["Göbekli Tepe — and on the same slope today", "In layer III of Göbekli Tepe, Hordeum cf. spontaneum, that is, wild barley, has been identified: fragments corresponding to an estimated 16 whole grains. Reinder Neef measured the grains; their being flat (0.7–1.4 mm) and broadly furrowed points to wild barley. The same report also records today’s vegetation: the slopes of the Göbekli Tepe plateau are still covered with a grass steppe dominated by wild barley. So this plant has stood on the same slope for eleven and a half thousand years."], ["Mardin — a wart ritual", "In the rural villages of Mardin, barley grains are buried under the soil after certain special prayers have been recited over them, in the belief that this will make the warts on the body disappear. Bird cherry and grapevine are also used within the same belief. The local name is “ceh”."], ["Şırnak — folk medicine", "Boiling barley seed and drinking it is used for bowel inflammation and constipation; its local names are “ca” and “ce”."], ["Mardin — craft", "Barley stalks are used to make baskets and drinking straws. They also appear in the making of a musical instrument called “temir” or “kendırge”: it is held between the teeth and blown to make a sound similar to a zurna."]])
_f('sarmasik', uses=[["Europe — expectorant", "Ivy leaf extract is one of the best-selling forms of herbal medicine for productive cough in Europe: it is sold without prescription as syrup, drops and effervescent tablets. The European Medicines Agency took the leaf into a monograph in 2009 and placed the indication under the heading \"well-established use\" — that is, according to the agency the use rests on clinical evidence, not only on a history of use. The indication in the monograph is a single sentence: an expectorant in productive cough."], ["Türkiye — official list", "It is in the TİTCK Medicinal Plant List with its leaf; the monograph gives the part used as \"leaves\" and lists the safety warnings in a separate section. In Türkiye, cough syrups containing ivy leaf extract are found in pharmacies."], ["Anatolia", "Ivy is distributed in the damp forests and gardens of Türkiye; its leaf, clinging to walls and tree trunks, stays green all year. The medicinal use of the leaf comes more from European phytotherapy than from Anatolian folk medicine."]])
_f('karniyarik', uses=[["India and Iran — a source of fibre", "When the seed husk comes into contact with water it forms a gel that swells to many times its volume; the traditional use also rests on this physical property. Most of the world’s production comes from the Gujarat region of India."], ["Europe — approved use in constipation", "The European Medicines Agency took the seed and the seed husk into separate monographs and in both placed the indication of habitual constipation under the heading \"well-established use\". Besides constipation, the monographs also list as an indication conditions in which keeping the stool soft is medically necessary."], ["Türkiye — official list", "The TİTCK monograph gives the part used as \"seeds, seed husks (testa epidermis)\" and lists the warnings; as literature it cites the EMA 2013 monograph."]])
_f('barutagaci', uses=[["Europe — short-term laxative", "The European Medicines Agency monograph places the bark in the \"well-established use\" category and keeps the indication narrow: short-term use in occasional constipation. The substances that give the effect are hydroxyanthracene derivatives (calculated as glucofrangulin A); they increase movement by stimulating the bowel wall."], ["Türkiye — official list", "The TİTCK monograph gives the part used as \"stem and branch bark\", and its list of warnings is longer than the EMA’s: not to be used for more than two weeks, not recommended under 12, not to be used in pregnancy and while breastfeeding because of the genotoxicity risk of some anthranoids, and to be used under the supervision of a doctor or pharmacist trained in herbal treatment."], ["European Union — medicine and food supplement are treated separately", "In this plant the line between medicine and food supplement is sharp. On the medicine side its monograph is in force. On the food side, the European Food Safety Authority announced in 2018 that it could not set a safe daily intake for hydroxyanthracene derivatives: evidence of genotoxicity was found for emodin, aloe-emodin and the structurally similar danthron, and attention was drawn to epidemiological data pointing to a link between laxative use and colorectal cancer risk. Following this, EU Regulation 2021/468 banned aloe-emodin, emodin and danthron in food; and it placed preparations made from alder buckthorn bark containing hydroxyanthracene derivatives on the Union scrutiny list. The distinction needs stressing: this is not a ban on the medicine. What is restricted is taking it through food supplements without a time limit."], ["Where the name comes from", "The name \"barut ağacı\" (gunpowder tree) has nothing to do with medicine: the charcoal obtained from its wood was one of the most sought-after charcoals for making black powder. There is no link between the plant’s medicinal use and its name."]])
_f('guneyafrikasardunyasi', uses=[["South Africa — its origin", "The plant is native to the upland grasslands of South Africa and Lesotho; its root was used in local folk medicine for respiratory complaints. It was carried to Europe at the beginning of the 20th century and today is among cold products."], ["Europe — the common cold", "The European Medicines Agency monograph places the indication under the heading \"traditional use\": relief of the symptoms of the common cold. The monograph also gives doses for children — ages 3–5, 6–12 and over 12 are written separately — which is not something often met in traditional-use monographs."], ["Türkiye — official list", "The TİTCK monograph gives the part used as \"roots\", requires the drug to comply with the European Pharmacopoeia monograph (ref. 2264), and writes the hepatotoxicity warning as a separate item."]])

# --- İngilizce sembol sayfası olmayan motiflerin bitki sayfasındaki bağ cümleleri (motif adı, cümle) ---
MOTIF_NOT = {
 ('sandik', 'ceviz'): ('Chest', 'The tree of the dowry chest the motif tells of: according to the registration document of the Kahramanmaraş carved dowry chest, the chest is made from walnut wood, and the wood is first dried so that it does not warp.'),
 ('ibrik', 'sigla'): ('Ewer', 'Its bark is dried and burned as incense under the name “günlük”: what the ewer’s water is, the censer’s smoke is; both are part of the same ceremonial cleansing.'),
 ('ibrik', 'gul'): ('Ewer', 'The rose water sprinkled from the rose-water flask is the second vessel on the same tray as the ewer.'),
 ('tarak', 'kenevir'): ('Comb', 'Before it goes into weaving, hemp fibre is combed on a comb with iron teeth: the tool that gives the motif its name is the tool that prepares the thread itself.'),
 ('tarak', 'keten'): ('Comb', 'Flax fibre goes through the same process too; the comb is where weaving begins.'),
 ('sacbagi', 'kina'): ('Hair tie', 'The plant of the wedding preparations the motif tells of: on the henna night henna is applied to the bride’s hands; henna marks devotion and farewell.'),
 ('kus', 'okseotu'): ('Bird', 'It takes its name from “ökse”, the birdlime made from its sticky berries and used to catch birds [5]: the oldest practice binding bird and plant together.'),
 ('kus', 'kusburnu'): ('Bird', 'The plant whose fruit is left to the birds in winter and whose name is also tied to the bird.'),
 ('yilan', 'karayilan'): ('Snake', 'It came into Turkish under the name “yılan kökü” (snake root); in North America its root was used against snakebite: the plant’s counterpart to the duality of poison and antidote in the motif.'),
 ('ejder', 'ejderkani'): ('Dragon', 'The drug whose name the motif shares: the dark red resin of the Socotra dragon tree appears in Arabic sources under the name “dragon’s blood”.'),
}
_f('camalonga', safety='It is very poisonous. It leads to vomiting, slowing of the pulse, heart block and fatal rhythm disorders. According to the international poison information monograph, the kernel of a single fruit can kill a child, and about ten of them an adult [4]. Because it is grown as an ornamental in Türkiye, care should be taken that children cannot reach the fruit. If poisoning is suspected, call 114 or 112 (in Türkiye).')
_f('acibiber', local='Pul biber, isot (Şanlıurfa), acı biber, arnavut biberi. The TİTCK monograph says "kırmızı biber".')
_f('ravent', local='Ravent, ışgın. ⚠️ The name "ışgın" is used in Anatolia for Rheum ribes — an entirely different species whose stalk is eaten in Eastern Anatolia. The plant in this record is not that one.')
_f('ucgul', local='Üçgül, kırmızı yonca, çayır tırfılı, çayır dutu, çayır üçgülü (TİTCK); tırfıl, yonca.')
_f('kirmizikantaron', other='The TİTCK monograph gives the Latin name in the broad sense: Centaurium erythraea Rafn s. l., including C. majus and C. suffruticosum; its synonyms are Erythraea centaurium, C. umbellatum, C. minus. In English centaury. ⚠️ It is not even related to **kantaron** (Hypericum perforatum) in our catalog: that one is from the Hypericaceae family, this one from the Gentianaceae.',
   confusion='⚠️ It is not the same thing as **kantaron** (Hypericum perforatum) in our catalog, and not even related to it. Yellow St John’s wort has yellow flowers, and its leaves look dotted when held up to the light; red centaury’s flower is pink-red and star-shaped. Their uses and interaction profiles are entirely different.')
_f('sinirliot', confusion='Broad-leaved plantain (Plantago major) of the same genus grows side by side with it everywhere and gets confused with it; the distinguishing feature is the leaf shape — this one’s leaf is long and spear-like, P. major’s leaf is broad and egg-shaped. **Karnıyarık** (psyllium, Plantago ovata) in our catalog, for its part, is of the same genus but is another plant whose seed is used and which works through its fibre effect; the EU monographs deal with the two separately.')
_f('hindiba', confusion='⚠️ It is confused with **karahindiba** (dandelion, Taraxacum officinale) in our catalog: the same family but a separate genus. Dandelion’s flower is yellow and single; chicory’s is blue and many on a branched stem. In addition, the leafy chicory varieties (radicchio, Belgian endive) are the same species but have been bred as vegetables; the subject of the monograph is the root.')
_f('kutsaldevedikeni', confusion='⚠️ Its name gets confused with devedikeni (milk thistle, Silybum marianum, the liver plant); the two are separate genera, with separate monographs and separate indications. Many species of spiny daisy-family plants look alike; a pharmacy product should be preferred. ⚠️ Because the plant’s synonym is Centaurea benedicta, **it is also confused with the cornflower (Centaurea) species**; those are separate plants and are not within the scope of this monograph.')
